WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.219
As facial reenactment videos are improving
at a rapid pace, it is getting easier and

00:00:09.219 --> 00:00:14.969
easier to create video impersonations of other
people by transferring our gestures onto their

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:15.969
faces.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:19.950
We have recently discussed a technique that
is able to localize the modified regions within

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:24.940
these videos, however, this technique was
limited to human facial reenactment.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.690
That is great, but what about the more general
case with manipulated photos?

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:33.559
Well, do not worry for second, because this
new learning-based algorithm can look at any

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:36.860
image and highlight the regions that were
tampered with.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.480
It can detect image splicing, which means
that we take part of a different image and

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.140
add it to this one.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.359
Or, copying an object and pasting it into
the image elsewhere.

00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:53.550
Or, removing an object from a photo and filling
in the hole with meaningful information harvested

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.980
from the image.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.769
This we also refer to as image inpainting,
and this is something that we also use often

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:02.940
to edit our thumbnail images that you see
here on YouTube.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.320
Believe it or not, it can detect all of these
cases.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.500
And it uses a two-stream convolutional neural
network to accomplish this.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.450
So what does this mean exactly?

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.000
This means a learning algorithm that looks
at one, the color data of the image to try

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.200
to find unnatural contrast changes along edges
and silhouettes, and two, the noise information

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.080
within the image as well and see how they
relate to each other.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.560
Typically, if the image has been tampered
with, either the noise, or the color data

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.650
is disturbed, or, it may also be that they
look good one by one, but the relation of

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:37.990
the two has changed.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.920
The algorithm is able to detect these anomalies
too.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.970
As many of the images we see on the internet
are either resized or compressed, or both,

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.510
it is of utmost importance that the algorithm
does not look at compression artifacts and

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.990
thinks that the image has been tampered with.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:57.160
This is something that even humans struggle
with on a regular basis, and this is luckily

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.270
not the case with this algorithm.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.140
This is great because a smart attacker may
try to conceal their mistakes by recompressing

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.270
an image and thereby adding more artifacts
to it.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.929
It's not going to fool this algorithm.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:13.611
However, as you Fellow Scholars pointed out
in the comments of a previous episode, if

00:02:13.611 --> 00:02:18.770
we have a neural network that is able to distinguish
forged images, with a little modification,

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:23.420
we can perhaps turn it around and use it as
a discriminator to help training a neural

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.390
network that produces better forgeries.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.920
What do you think about that?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.200
It is of utmost importance that we inform
the public that these tools exist.

